Pressure-induced bimetallic carbon nanotubes from metal–organic frameworks as optimized bifunctional electrocatalysts for water splitting
Abstract Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have attracted great interest in numerous applications due to their excellent electronic and structural properties. However, harsh synthesis conditions and high energy consumption limit the further application of CNTs. Herein, we developed a facile and innovative strategy to generate CNTs by physically pressurizing metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and then pyrolyzing. The synthesized bimetallic nitrogen/sulfur double-doped carbon nanotubes are denoted as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{P}\)
\((x=0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9)\). The introduction of pressure not only revolutionized the morphology of the electrocatalysts, but also enhanced the activity of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in alkaline solution. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{P}\) exhibits superior OER and HER performances in \(1.0\ \mathrm{mol\cdot L^{-1}}\). KOH solution with overpotentials of 364 and \(346\ \mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\), which are 28 and \(5\ \mathrm{mV}\) lower than unpressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\).
Keywords Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs); Carbon nanotubes (CNTs); Bifunctional; Pressurizing; Oxygen evolution reaction (OER); Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER)
## 1 Introduction
The acceleration of the world's industrialization process has led to an uncontrolled increase in carbon dioxide emissions, leading to a steady flow of global climate problems [1, 2]. Therefore, the development of a low-carbon economy is imminent. Electrocatalytic water splitting to produce hydrogen is one of the most effective and sustainable avenues to reduce carbon emissions [3, 4]. Although the existing of commercial alkaline electrolyzers provides an effective method for large-scale production of high-purity hydrogen, its efficiency is still unsatisfactory [5, 6]. This is mainly due to its two electrode reactions: the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at the cathode and the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at the anode have a higher overpotential [7–9]. Thus, high-efficiency bifunctional electrocatalysts are required to lower the reaction energy barrier and improve energy efficiency [10, 11]. So far, the most advanced OER and HER electrocatalysts are
precious metals and their compounds. The precious metal platinum \((\mathrm{Pt})\) is the most active HER element, and the precious metal oxides \(\mathrm{IrO}_{x}\) and \(\mathrm{RuO}_{x}\) are the benchmark electrocatalysts for OER [12–14]. In recent years, in order to break the limitations of scarce and expensive noble metals, non-noble-metal-based electrocatalysts have been investigated [15–17]. Especially, the first-row transition metals, such as Ni, Co and Fe, have been reported to significantly improve electrocatalytic activities [18–20]. However, ordinary transition metal-based materials still cannot meet the requirements of high-efficiency electrocatalysts: large specific surface areas, high porosity, optimized active site distribution and high charge transfer ability [21, 22]. Thus, it is still a challenge to explore more satisfactory electrocatalysts for water splitting.
The discovery of carbon nanotubes (CNTs) has stimulated active and multidisciplinary research in the past few decades across nanotechnology, electronics, energy, and environmental areas [23]. This low-dimensional carbon nanostructure has attracted extensive interest and demonstrated great promise in numerous applications [24–26]. Compared with zero-dimensional (0D), two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) nanomaterials, one-dimensional (1D) nanotubes exhibit high electron mobility performance, more active sites, greater electrical conductivity and higher structural stability [27, 28]. By combining the advantages of FeCoNi alloy with N-CNTs, Li et al. [29] reported bamboolike N-doped carbon nanotubes FeCoNi@N-CNTs. CNTs have large active surface areas, ultrahigh content of graphitic carbon and abundant active metal- \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{N}_{x}\) species, thus demonstrating better electrocatalytic performance for OER. Currently developed synthesis strategies of CNTs include arc discharge, laser ablation and chemical vapor deposition methods, etc. [30–32]. Despite great advancements in their synthesis, high costs and high energy dissipation continue to limit CNTs for further applications [33]. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) possess high surface areas, tunable porosity and controllable structures [34–36]. When used as electrode materials for catalyzing HER and OER, MOF-based materials can be functionally designed through self-assembly according to target characteristics [37–39]. In addition, MOFs can serve as outstanding templates to design various nanostructures by post-processing, such as heteroatom doped porous carbons, metal chalcogenides, metal nitrides [40, 41]. These materials not only show excellent electronic conductivity, but also inherit the original large specific surface areas and rich pore structure, making them competitive candidates for electrocatalysis [42, 43]. For instance, Xia et al. [44] synthesized N-doped carbon nanotube frameworks (NCNTFs) materials by a simple thermal treatment of purple ZIF-67 particles at \(700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an \(\mathrm{Ar}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) atmosphere. NCNTFs exhibit higher electrocatalytic activity and
stability for oxygen reduction and evolution than commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) electrocatalyst.
To date, a number of efforts have been made aiming to investigate the effects of pressure on MOFs [45]. Through the pressure-induced phase transformations, MOFs will decrease interparticle separation distance and consolidate the particles into a denser configuration, due to they are highly compressible [46, 47]. Furthermore, the mechanical pressure has significant influence on MOF structure, which in turn affect their gas separation performance [48]. Inspired by the previous achievements, we conjecture that the electrocatalytic performance of MOFs can be improved by mechanical pressurization.
Herein, we report a facile and innovative strategy to generate CNTs by physically pressurizing metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and then pyrolyzing. The synthesized bimetallic nitrogen/sulfur co-doped carbon nanotubes were denoted as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{P}\) \((x=0.5\), 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9)\). The introduction of pressure not only completely changed the morphology of the electrocatalysts, but also improved the activity of OER and HER as well as good durability in alkaline solution. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{P}\) displayed the best OER and HER performances in \(1.0\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) KOH solution with overpotentials of 364 and \(346~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which are 28 and \(5~\mathrm{mV}\) lower than the unpressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\). These results suggest that there is a great potential for the development of practical bifunctional electrocatalysts by introducing physical pressure and regulating metal content.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of electrocatalysts
## 2.1.1 Synthesis of \(F e_{x}C o_{1-x}M O F s\)
\(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with molar ratios of 5:5, 6:4, 7:3, 8:2, 9:1 (total moles is \(80\;\mathrm{mmol}\)) were firstly dissolved in dimethyl sulfoxide \((320\;\mathrm{ml})\) (Solution I). \(\mathrm{NaN}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}\) \((160\;\mathrm{mmol})\) and hexamethylenetetramine \((80\;\mathrm{mmol})\) were dissolved in deionized water \((200\;\mathrm{ml})\) (Solution II). Then, Solution I and Solution II were mixed and stirred at room temperature for \(6{-}8\mathrm{~h~}\). The brown crystals were collected and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\).
## 2.1.2 Synthesis of \(F e_{x}C o_{1-x}P s\)
\(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{MOFs}\) \((2\mathrm{~g~})\) were pressurized with a force of \(5\;\mathrm{MPa}\) to form a flake. The obtained flake was transferred into a ceramic boat and calcined at \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). The pyrolyzed carbon materials were soaked in \(0.5\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\;\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) for pickling to remove impurities on the surface. Then, the material was washed
with deionized water and dried at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\) Ps. Finally, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{Ps}\) were placed in a tube furnace and heated to \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\) to prepare \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}@\) Os.
## 2.2 Electrochemical measurement
The electrochemical measurements were conducted at a Zahner Zennium electrochemical workstation by a typical three-electrode system in \(1.0\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) KOH. A graphite rod was used as a counter electrode, while an \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) electrode was used as a reference electrode. The working electrode was prepared as follows: In ink preparation, \(5~\mathrm{mg}\) of sample was placed in a mixed solvent of \(1\,\mathrm{\ml}\) of deionized water and propanol (3:1, V/V), and sonicated with an ultrasonic machine for \(1\!-\!2\;\mathrm{h}\) until the powder was dispersed, then \(30~\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of Nafion \((5\mathrm{\wt}\%)\) was added to the liquid, and sonicated for 30 minutes to obtain a uniform ink, and in electrode preparation, after polishing and cleaning the glassy carbon electrode \((\mathrm{GCE}\,0.0706\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) ), 5 \(\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of ink was added on its surface for better readability.
Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was designed to activate the catalyst with a scan rate of \(500\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) for 1000 cycles. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was performed to evaluate OER performance at a scan rate of \(5\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) . The frequency of the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) curve was \(10\ \mathrm{kHz}{-}0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}\) . All potentials \((E)\) are converted into a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) according to the following formula: \(E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}+\) \(0.197+0.0591\mathrm{pH}\) (here \(\mathrm{pH}=14\) ).
## 2.3 Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements were recorded on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with a \(\mathrm{Cu}\) \(\mathrm{K}\upalpha\) radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) data were acquired from a Thermal Fisher Scientific K-Alpha electron spectrometer. The morphologies of the samples were characterized by the scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, Vltra55, Carl Zeiss) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2100). The specific surface areas were measured by Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) on a Micrometrics ASAP 2010 system. The samples were
degassed at \(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) before the measurement and \(\Nu_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms were obtained at \(77\,\mathrm{\,K}\) .
## 3 Results and discussion
\(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}\) \((x=0.5\) , 0.6, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9) were successfully synthesized through pressurizing and pyrolyzing, as shown in Scheme 1 (see the Support Information for details). As shown in Fig. 1, through pressurization, the morphology of the electrocatalyst was transformed from the original carbon nanoblock into carbon nanotube. Furthermore, we found that the performance of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}@\) O obtained by oxidation of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}\) was further improved.
The microscopic morphology of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{P}\) was gained by SEM. The inset in Fig. 1a shows that the unpressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-MOF}\) has a sharp rectangular parallelepiped appearance, which is not much different from the unpressurized MOFs (Fig. 1b). SEM image of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) under high magnification is shown in Fig. 1a; the morphology is irregular block with a few short carbon nanotubes on the surface. The other proportions of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\) are also the same as its appearance (Fig. S1). However, after pyrolysis, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{P}\) completely turned into curved carbon nanotubes (as shown in Fig. 1c). Figure S2 proves that the remaining proportions of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}\) have also undergone the same change. This outward may be due to the transformation in the internal microscopic morphology of pressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{MOFs}\) under the action of external pressure. After high-temperature pyrolysis, the deformation fully displayed, and the nanoblocks directly transformed into nanotubes. The surface of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x^{-}}\mathrm{P}@\) O produced by oxidation is covered with wrinkles, and the diameter of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{P}@\) O is \(70{-}100\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figs. 1d and S3). Theoretically, the carbon nanotubes formed by transition metal with the uneven surface characteristics help to increase the exposed area of the catalysts and promote the rapid diffusion of the electrolyte [49]. TEM images of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{P}@\) O are shown in Fig. 1e, f, where the darker area indicates that metal nanoparticles with a diameter of about \(20\ \mathrm{nm}\) are embedded at the end of the nanotubes. This structure will promote effective electron transfer and
enhance the stability of the catalyst in the electrocatalytic process [50]. Therefore, it is expected that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) will show better performance in OER and HER than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\).
XRD patterns of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) are shown in Fig. 2, which confirm the presence of the metal/metal oxide/metal carbide composition. For \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\), the peak at \(26.2^{\circ}\) is attributed to (002) plane of FeC alloy (PDF No. 03-0411), and the peak at \(31.2^{\circ}\) is indexed to (440) plane of Co species (PDF No. 01-1338). This means that iron and cobalt elements have been successfully doped into carbon nanotubes. And it can be seen that the metal peak of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) is sharper than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\), which also corresponds to the most excellent catalytic performance of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\). Figures S4, S5 show XRD patterns of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOFs}\) and pressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{-MOFs}\). And according to Fig. S6, the peaks of pressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-MOF}\) are significantly weakened after pressurizing, indicating that mechanical pressure creates smaller particle size and tighter structure. At the same time, PXRD patterns of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{-P@O}\) confirmed the existence of Fe and Co elements (Fig. S7).
Measuring the pore structure and specific surface area by BET method is one of the important methods to detect performance. Figures S8, S9 depict the nitrogen adsorption isotherms of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{1-x}\mathrm{-P@O}\), and the surface area and pore size values are tabulated in Table S1. The results show that the BET surface area and pore size of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) are \(327.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(11.6\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively. But the BET surface area and pore size of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) are only \(302.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(6.0\ \mathrm{nm}\). This illustrates that physical pressurization can efficiently increase the pore volume of electrocatalysts, thereby enhancing the OER and HER performance. The larger specific surface areas and unique mesoporous structure can efficiently promote the diffusion of reactants and close contact between the reactants, and more importantly, expose more active sites, thus improving the electrocatalytic performance [51]. The graphitization feature of carbon in the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) samples was investigated by Raman spectrum. Two prominent peaks at 1350 and \(1580\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in Raman spectra correspond to the typical D (disordered or defect carbon) and G bands (graphitized \(\mathrm{sp}^{2}\) carbon), respectively. The density of defects is correlated to the ratio of intensities of D and G
bands \((I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})\) [52]. Thus, \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) is applied to measure the surface/edge defect and degree of disorder. The higher \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/\) \(I_{\mathrm{G}}\) value reveals defect-rich sites in nanomaterials. The ratio values of \(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}\) increased as follows: \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) \((0.57)<\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) \((0.79)<\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) (0.81), indicating the pressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) sample possessed more defect sites.
In order to analyze the chemical environment of the material, XPS measurements were performed on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) , which confirmed the presence of iron, cobalt, oxygen, sulfur, nitrogen, and carbon (Fig. 3a). As revealed by XPS survey
In order to analyze the chemical environment of the material, XPS measurements were performed on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) , which confirmed the presence of iron, cobalt, oxygen, sulfur, nitrogen, and carbon (Fig. 3a). As revealed by XPS survey spectrum, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) contains \(1.1\;\mathrm{at}\%\) , \(0.94\;\mathrm{at}\%\) , 7.16 \(\mathrm{at}\%\) , \(0.48\;\mathrm{at}\%\) , \(2.69\;\mathrm{at}\%\) and \(87.62\;\mathrm{at}\%\) of Fe, Co, O, S, N and C, respectively. In Fe \(2\mathrm{p}\) spectrum of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) , the typical core-level signals located at Fe \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) and Fe \(2{\bf p}_{3/}\) 2, respectively. Fe \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\) can be deconvoluted to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) oxidation state at \(711.2\ \mathrm{eV}\) , and the peak at \(724.1~\mathrm{eV}\) is \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) [53] (Fig. 3b). XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}\) shows the presence of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) . \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) peak can be deconvoluted to \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) at \(795.6~\mathrm{eV}\) , and the fitted peak at \(780.2~\mathrm{eV}\) represents \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) , with the two satellite peaks located at 784.9 and \(802.8~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively (Fig. 3c). The deconvolution of O 1s spectrum shows that there are oxygen vacancies \((531.5\;\mathrm{eV})\) and metal–oxygen bonds \((529.7~\mathrm{eV})\) , in which oxygen vacancies are dominant. It is well known that oxygen vacancies can increase the conductivity of electrocatalysts [54]. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) has stronger oxygen vacancies than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) thus exhibiting better activity in catalysis (Fig. 3d). S \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\) and S \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) peaks of \(\mathrm{S}^{2-}\) are concentrated at 168.1 and \(169.1~\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 3e). As for N 1s, the bonding state of N in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-}\) \(\mathrm{P@O}\) was analyzed by high-resolution XPS. The spectrum is deconvoluted into two peaks (Fig. 3f): pyridine nitrogen and graphite nitrogen are located at 397.9 and \(400.7~\mathrm{eV}\) , respectively. It is worth noting that pyridine divalent nitrogen can coordinate with metal atoms through the metal–N–C structure, thereby optimizing the local electronic structure and promoting the electrochemical process. In addition, C 1s spectrum confirmed the presence of C–C
\((284.4~\mathrm{eV})\) and \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\) \((286.6~\mathrm{eV})\) in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) (Fig. S10). In order to explore the influence of electronic structure on electrocatalytic performance, XPS measurements were also performed on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}.\) - \(\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) (Fig. S11). Compared with \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peak of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) are negative, respectively, shifted 0.04, 0.23, 0.99 and \(0.43~\mathrm{eV}\) . However, \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) peaks shifted to higher binding energy in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.5}\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}.\) - \(\mathrm{P}@0\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.2}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) , corresponding to 781.2, 780.3, 780.9 and \(780.8~\mathrm{eV}\) . Therefore, Fe and Co in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) form the strongest synergistic effect and contribute to the improvement of catalyst performance.
