For HER, the activity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}\) was detected under similar conditions. Figure 4c shows HER polarization curves of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) , \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}.\) - \(\mathrm{P}@0\) . \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) showed very poor HER activity and made negligible contribution to HER beyond \(\approx~-\) \(351~\mathrm{mV}\) . It was found that the overpotential required to deliver the current density of \(-10~\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) for \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}\) is only \(346~\mathrm{mV}\) , which is \(5~\mathrm{mV}\) smaller than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) . Figure 4e illustrates that the overpotential of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) at \(10~\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) is much smaller than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) in OER and HER. Besides, a lower Tafel slope \((114~\mathrm{mV{\cdot}d e c}^{-1})\) for \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{P}@0\) relative to \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) \((187~\mathrm{mV{\cdot}d e c}^{-1})\) was also observed, suggesting faster HER kinetics (Fig. 4d). The electrochemical active
surface area (ECSA) of the catalysts was evaluated by double-layer capacitance \((C_{\mathrm{dl}})\), so as to compare the active sites of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\). As shown in Fig. 4f, \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) (2.48 \(\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) obtained from cyclic voltammograms (Fig. S13) is larger than those of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) (1.57 \(\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)). And \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) highlights the importance of the oxidation process to promote catalytic activity. Besides better OER performance, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) also exhibits satisfactory electrocatalytic performance for HER. Therefore, an electrolyzer in a two-electrode system with the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) as both the anode and cathode were assembled in an alkaline aqueous electrolyte solution. Figure S14 shows that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) is able to deliver the overall water splitting with a catalytic current density of \(10\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) at a cell voltage of \(1.96\mathrm{~V~}\) in \(1.0\;\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) KOH electrolyte solution. On the contrary, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) shows extremely poor performance of overall water splitting and could not reach the current density of \(10\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) under the same conditions. Moreover, EIS was performed to evaluate the charge transfer resistance \((R_{\mathrm{ct}})\) of the catalyst (Fig. S15).
As shown in Fig. 5a, b and Figs. S16–S19, the effect of Fe content on HER and OER performance was further investigated. The results show that the overpotential and Tafel slope of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) are the minimum for both HER and OER. When the doping ratio of Fe to Co is 6:4, the electrocatalyst exhibits the most outstanding catalytic activity and the least resistance to electron transport. \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) is a high-performance electrocatalyst for HER and OER with superior activity in alkaline media, which is superior to most reported bifunctional catalysts (Table S4). Also, the overpotential of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.7}\mathrm{Co}_{0.3}\mathrm{-P@O}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.2}\mathrm{-P@O}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.9}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}\mathrm{-P@O}\) gradually reduced after pressurizing and oxidation. Stability is another important parameter for evaluating the practical value of electrocatalysts. 1000 continuous cycles were performed in the HER and OER potential windows at a scan rate of \(500\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\), respectively. At the end of cycling, the two polarization curves only display a small current loss (Figs. S20, S21), indicating that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) has much better stability. Also, SEM image (Fig. S22) shows that the morphology of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) maintained after OER, indicating the structure stability of the electrocatalyst. The voltage–time \((\nu{-}t)\) test further
confirms the long-term stability of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\). As a result, the overpotential of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) remained almost unchanged within \(12~\mathrm{h}\), and the rate of change is only \(0.01\%\). In contrast, for \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) electrocatalysts, their overpotentials experienced greater fluctuations after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) (Figs. S23–S26).
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a bifunctional electrocatalyst that can simultaneously improve the performance of HER and OER by pressurizing, pyrolyzing, oxidizing and adjusting the metal ratio of the bimetallic S/N codoped MOFs. The overpotential of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P}\) at a current density of \(10~\mathrm{mA\cdot cm}^{-2}\) in OER and HER is 28 and \(5~\mathrm{mV}\) smaller than that of unpressurized \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\). Moreover, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.6}\mathrm{Co}_{0.4}\mathrm{-P@O}\) also possesses the favorable reaction kinetics and the longest current stability. These outcomes can be largely attributed to the change in the appearance of the electrocatalysts caused by mechanical pressure, which expanded the specific surface area and exposed more active sites of the sample. This strategy may be extended to manufacture of MOFs-derived electrocatalysts for energy conversion and storage-related applications.
